# Ensure consistent line endings across platforms.
#
# LF is the default for every text file, not an allowlist of extensions. The
# allowlist approach failed twice: .py and .json were uncovered until 59347a9,
# and .txt was uncovered until this commit -- each time, Git for Windows'
# core.autocrlf=true silently rewrote those files on checkout while the stored
# blobs stayed LF, making the same source produce different bytes per platform.
# Every new file extension would have repeated it.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content and leave it alone. The repository
# is currently 100% text (md, yml, sh, py, json, txt, ps1, .example, LICENSE),
# so nothing is at risk; a future binary asset needs an explicit `-text` rule.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rules kept for documentation value -- they say which formats this
# repository actually ships. They are redundant with the default above.
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
# .py and .json were previously uncovered. Git for Windows ships
# core.autocrlf=true in its system gitconfig, so every Windows checkout rewrote
# them to CRLF while the stored blobs stayed LF. That made byte-level
# comparison of generated artifacts platform-dependent: the same source
# produced different bytes on Windows and Linux. All tracked .py/.json blobs
# were already LF, so these rules change checkout behavior only, never content.
*.py text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
